<section id="workload-price-guides" class="mt-8">
  <h2>Workload price guides: ranking, answer length and routing</h2>
  <p>Source inspected 14 November 2026: <a href={`${pinned}src/data/insights/2026-11-14.json`}>frozen AIRH catalogue insight snapshot</a>, specifically priceSpread.all entries mistral-large-4-0 and gemini-3.5-flash-lite. The source cache records generation at {d.cacheGeneratedAt} and input SHA-256 {d.inputSha256}. That hash identifies the original input cache, not this derived receipt.</p>
  <p>These two rows were chosen because their input and output rates cross. No claim about their representativeness, quality or present availability follows. Source labels are preserved verbatim: {d.models.map(m=>`${m.name}: ${m.source}; imported ${m.checked}`).join('; ')}. No fresh vendor price verification took place. An import time is not a verification time. This uses existing catalogue records and admits no new third-party dataset; source-policy and rights decisions stay with the data owners.</p>
  <p>All amounts are US dollars. Rates per million tokens: {d.models.map(m=>`${m.name}: input ${m.input}, output ${m.output}`).join('; ')}. Terms used in these guides are definitions for the worked method: input/output are the two charged token categories; a denominator is the divisor of a rate; routing chooses the model; escalation is a second-stage call; break-even means equal costs; acceptance means satisfying the written task rule. Recommendations about record-keeping are editorial proposals, not measured effects.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/blog/blended-price-ranking-reversal/">Blended ranking</a>: r = output/input. Weighted dollars per million combined tokens = (inputRate + r × outputRate)/(1 + r). Ratios 0.1, 0.25, 1, 3 are assumptions. Equality solves 0.70 + 2.10r = 0.30 + 2.50r, giving r = 1. This also documents the existing site's 1:3 convention from <a href={`${pinned}src/data/transparency.ts`}>calc-blended-price</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="/blog/answer-length-budget-range/">Answer length</a>: cost = jobs × (inputTokens × inputRate + outputTokens × outputRate)/1,000,000. Jobs = 10,000; input = 3,000; output = 500, 2,000 or 8,000. These are scenarios with no probabilities or measurements. Increment per additional output token on each job = outputRate/100 dollars.</li>
    <li><a href="/blog/two-stage-routing-break-even/">Routing</a>: C1 = first-stage cost/job, C2 = direct alternative cost/job, f = assumed escalated share. Queue cost = 10,000 × (C1 + f × C2). Break-even f = 1 − C1/C2 = {d.breakEvenFallbackShare}. Shares 0, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1 are assumed. Counts 3,000 input and 500 output at both stages. This does not measure failures or imply that either model is better.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Reproduction: node scripts/insights/workload-price-guides.mjs --check checks the complete derived frozen file src/data/insights/2026-11-14-workload-prices.json byte for byte. Values are rounded to six decimal places only for storage/display; break-even uses unrounded call costs. No original snapshot is overwritten. No provider requests, quality tests or price-history claims. No Artificial Analysis or Arena data.</p>
  <p>Exclusions: caching, batch discounts, reasoning beyond the assumed output count, tools, retries, taxes, judging and human review. Equal token counts across models are assumed, not evidence of equal tokenisation. Live offers and applicable conditions require separate verification. The three guides' numerical findings are arithmetic on saved records; their workflow examples are proposals for a reader's own trial.</p>
</section>
